e Kisii highlands after pressure from Luo and passage through Kalenjin/Maasai territory. The Gusii are renowned for their soapstone sculptures (chigware, Tabaka), the eight-stringed bass lyre (obokano—the largest such lyre in Kenya), and their historic reputation as formidable warriors. Colonial encounters were marked by punitive expeditions, suppression of cattle camps (ebisarate), and forced settlement dispersal (1913). The community maintains a decentralized, clan-based governance structure and practices male circumcision as a central rite of passage. Contemporary Gusii have significant diaspora populations in the United States (notably Minnesota), United Kingdom, Canada, Australia, New Zealand, and South Africa.",', 'community': 'kisii', 'section': 'section_0', 'relevance': 1.1000020503997803, 'source': 'Kisii.txt'}]}
