uo",\n    "language": "Dholuo",\n    "homeland": "Piny Luo / Nam Lolwe (Lake Victoria)",\n    "alternative_terms": [\n      "Jonagi/Onagi (refers to removal of six lower teeth as rite of passage)",\n      "Joramogi / Nyikwaramogi (Ramogi\'s heirs)",\n      "Nilotic Kavirondo (colonial designation)"\n    ]\n  },\n  "aliases": [\n    "Joluo",\n    "Nilotic Kavirondo",\n    "Southern Luo",\n    "Ramogi\'s heirs"\n  ],\n  \n  "summary": "The Luo are a Western Nilotic ethnic group native to western Kenya and the Mara Region of northern Tanzania. They are Kenya\'s fourth-largest ethnic group (5.07M, ~10.6% of national population, 2019 census). They form the southernmost extension of the broader Luo peoples, a large ethnolinguistic family spanning South Sudan, Ethiopia, Uganda, Kenya, and Tanzania. Historically a cattle pastoralist, seed farmer, and fishing society, the Luo today are heavily represented in East Africa\'s intellectual, scientific, and professional classes. The community has produced outsized global figures: Barack Obama (44th US President), Lupita Nyong\'o (first black African Academy Award winner), Tom Mboya (Kenyan founding father), and Oginga Odinga (first Vice President). Luo political history is marked by early anti-colonial activism, Cold War-era marginalization, and sustained Kikuyu-Luo rivalry shaping Kenyan multiparty democracy. Culturally, the Luo are originators of benga and ohangla music. Unlike neighboring Nilotes (Kalenjin, Maasai), Luo do not practice male circumcision and historically removed six lower incisors as a distinctive ethnic marker.",', 'community': 'luo', 'relevance_score': 1.5523858070373535}], 'search_type': 'community_specific', 'raw_results': [{'content': '"history": {\n    "precolonial_polity": {\n      "system": "Decentralized, patriarchal, gerontocratic",\n      "basic_unit": "Family (father or mikayi/first wife or senior son)",\n      "clan": "Anyuola (patrilineal descent group)",\n      "village": "Gweng, headed by dodo/jaduong gweng (village elder)",\n      "sub_tribe": "Piny (tribal state), headed by ruoth (hereditary chieftaincy, merit-based, eldest son)",\n      "ruoth_installation": "Crowned by jabilo (diviner), who served as spiritual/military advisor",\n      "council": "Galamoro mar jodongo/jodong gweng (council of elders)",\n      "defense": "Osumba mirwayi (commander), part of council",\n      "inter_subtribe_relations": "Ad hoc; cooperation during calamities, war, drought, famine; sumo (sharing produce)"\n    },\n    "colonial_period": {\n      "early_contact": "Indirect until Uganda Railway; punitive expedition against Kager clan (1896, Ochieng Ger III), Maxim gun killed 200+",\n      "uyoma_resistance": "~300 killed by Charles Horbley (Bwana Obila Muruayi) during cattle confiscation for Uganda Railway workers",\n      "shift_to_cooperation": "Following clashes, Luo spiritual leaders advised cooperation; by 1900, Chief Odera provided 1500 porters for British expedition against Nandi",\n      "colonial_administration": "HQ at Mumias then Kisumu; traditional leaders replaced by colonial chiefs",\n      "missionary_activity": {\n        "sda": "Arthur Carscallen, Peter Nyambo (Malawi), Abraham Enns; first mission Gendia Hill (1906); first baptisms 21 May 1911; Carscallen reduced Dholuo to writing",\n        "catholic": "Active in Nyanza",\n        "cms_anglican": "Active in Northern Nyanza"\n      },\n      "westernization": "Accelerated 1910s–1930s; Odera Akang\'o (Ruoth of Gem) visited Kampala (1915), forced adoption of English schooling, dress, hygiene",\n      "resistance_movements": {\n        "mumboism": "c. 1913, Onyango Dunde; serpent goddess Mumbo; millennialist, anti-colonial, condemned colonial religion; suppressed 1920s–30s via deportation/imprisonment; spread among Luo and Kisii"\n      },\n      "early_political_organization": {\n        "piny_owacho": "Voice of the People; mass meetings (Dec 1921); protested Kipande, taxes, land alienation; alliance with Kiku